{
  "requirement": "When a mandatory input or dispatched model read is denied by source-access\npolicy, the assessment MUST abort and report the denied path and reason to its caller.\nIt MUST NOT return the denial to the model as a recoverable tool result or make\nanother model request for that assessment. An untracked-file diagnostic MUST\nexplain that the caller can stage the file with `git add` or configure a workspace\nallow rule; the harness MUST NOT stage or permit the file itself.\n\nThe aborted assessment MUST NOT write evidence or emit evidence JSON. Its usage\nrecord MUST identify the failure and any failed tool call. In a batch, other\nassessments MUST continue, including queued assessments, and their successful\nwrites MUST be retained. The command MUST return a nonzero exit status after\nthe batch finishes. Discovery and search MAY omit denied candidates without\naborting, but explicitly requesting a denied file or directory MUST abort the\naffected assessment.\nMalformed tool arguments and ordinary read failures MUST retain their existing\nrecoverable tool-error behavior.\n\nA dispatched model request naming a path outside the project root, before\nsymlinks are resolved, that no workspace allow rule or generated-output rule\npermits MUST be treated as an ordinary read failure rather than an abort: it\nMUST return an explanatory tool error, add no evidence input, and let the\nassessment continue. An explicit workspace disallow matching such a path MUST\nstill abort. A mandatory input naming such a path, and a request inside the\nproject whose symlink target leaves it, MUST still abort.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge that the sources satisfy `requirements.authoring.read_denial`. One edge case is untested and arguably out of scope; it is described at the end.\n\n**Policy denials become aborts**\n- `SourceAccess::path` and `file` in `src/authoring/access.rs` return `PermissionDenied` errors for these denials:\n  - an explicit `habeas.toml` disallow, via `check_disallow`, including alias and ancestor redirection;\n  - workspace credentials;\n  - generated evidence;\n  - a target that resolves outside the project;\n  - an untracked file or directory.\n- The untracked diagnostics say to use `git add` or add an explicit `habeas.toml` allow rule. The harness never stages anything.\n- Every mandatory-input read goes through `access.file` in `AssessmentSources::prepare`, `source_input` and the anchor handling in `src/authoring/inputs.rs`. Each propagates the error with `?`, so preparation fails before any model request.\n- In `assess` (`src/authoring/mod.rs`), a `PermissionDenied` error from `tools::dispatch` is recorded with `usage.tool(name, true)` and then returned. It is never turned into `call.error_result`, and no further request follows.\n- Non-denial errors still go back to the model as recoverable tool results. This covers malformed arguments, a missing file, an invalid pattern and bad ranges.\n\n**Outside-project requests**\n- `OutsideProject` is a dedicated error type, built only when the lexical, pre-symlink path leaves the root. It is checked after the disallow, allow, generated-output and credential branches.\n- `recoverable_outside_request`, applied in `dispatch`, turns it into an ordinary `Other` error, so the assessment continues and no input is recorded.\n- A matching disallow still aborts.\n- A request inside the project whose symlink target leaves it still aborts, via \"resolves outside the project\".\n- A mandatory input outside the project still aborts, because `prepare` propagates the `PermissionDenied` kind.\n\n**Batch behaviour**\n- `PendingAssessment::failed` and `prepare_assessment` finish the usage record with outcome `failed` and keep the failed-tool count.\n- `author_all_with_options` cancels only on `Interrupted`, so a denial fails only its own assessment. Other and queued assessments run and their writes are kept.\n- `main` returns an error, so the exit status is nonzero after the batch finishes.\n- No evidence is written for an aborted assessment.\n- Discovery and search (`list_files`, `search` and `references`) silently skip denied candidates. An explicit request for a denied path aborts.\n\n**Tests**\n- `access.rs` tests cover:\n  - disallow beating allow, including symlink alias targets;\n  - disallowed ancestors, `..` handling and credentials;\n  - the inherited `OUT_DIR` case.\n- `inputs.rs` tests cover:\n  - disallowed mandatory inputs;\n  - denied anchors;\n  - generated-evidence anchors omitted by default and aborting under an explicit disallow;\n  - untracked mandatory inputs with the `git add` hint.\n- `tools.rs` tests cover:\n  - generated evidence;\n  - untracked files and symlinks;\n  - allowed versus disallowed descendants;\n  - Cargo output disallow;\n  - the outside-project recoverable case, with a disallow still aborting;\n  - mandatory outside-project inputs aborting.\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs` checks that an untracked read via `habeas`, `ripgrep` or `list_files` aborts with:\n  - exactly one model request;\n  - empty stdout;\n  - unchanged evidence;\n  - a usage record showing `failed` and a tool failure.\n- `tests/authoring_batch.rs` checks that one denial leaves the other assessments to complete, including queued and in-flight ones, with a nonzero exit and the path in the diagnostic.\n- The CLI test `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` shows an ordinary missing-file read staying recoverable.\n\n**Remaining caveats (minor)**\n- `AssessmentSources::read_lines`, `read_selector` and `additional_definitions` carry no `read_denial` marker. They only forward denials from marked functions (`parse_file`, `source_input`).\n- `assess` tells policy denial from ordinary failure by `io::ErrorKind::PermissionDenied`. A genuine OS-level permission error on an unreadable directory (for example in `list_files`) would therefore also abort. This is an untested edge case, and I treated it as outside what the requirement meaningfully constrains.",
